---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import ControlRoomHeroVideo from '../components/ControlRoomHeroVideo.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Control Room"
  description="How mission control teams coordinate a space mission: roles, loops, decisions, and failure management."
>
  <section class="page-home-hero">
    <ControlRoomHeroVideo />
    <div class="page-home-hero-inner">
      <p class="page-home-hero-eyebrow">Flight Operations</p>
      <h1 class="page-home-hero-heading">Inside the control room</h1>
      <p class="page-home-hero-lead">
        Teams, loops, and callouts working in sync to keep missions inside safe limits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pb-24 md:px-8">
    <PageHero
      eyebrow="Mission context"
      title="How mission teams actually operate"
      subtitle="A mission does not run on one voice. It runs on tightly coordinated teams, disciplined communication, and rapid risk assessment."
    />
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <section class="space-y-5 text-slate-300">
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-white">How teams are structured</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          Flight control is usually split by subsystem expertise: guidance and navigation, propulsion, power, thermal,
          life support, communications, payloads, and mission timeline. A flight director integrates these streams into
          one coherent go/no-go decision path.
        </p>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          In crewed programs, discipline matters as much as technical depth: concise callouts, explicit confirmations,
          and strict escalation when data diverges from expected behavior.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-void-850/40 p-6 md:p-8">
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-white">Typical console responsibilities</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-400">
          <li><span class="text-nebula">•</span> Flight Director — final mission authority in the room</li>
          <li><span class="text-nebula">•</span> CAPCOM — primary voice link to crew</li>
          <li><span class="text-nebula">•</span> GNC — attitude, trajectory, and control law monitoring</li>
          <li><span class="text-nebula">•</span> PROP / EECOM — propulsion, consumables, thermal and power states</li>
          <li><span class="text-nebula">•</span> INCO / COMM — data paths, comm windows, and telemetry integrity</li>
          <li><span class="text-nebula">•</span> FAO / Timeline — mission plan execution and timeline adjustments</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-nebula/20 bg-nebula/5 p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-white">Decision rhythm during anomalies</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate-300">
        Detect anomaly → isolate subsystem impact → bound immediate risk → choose stable configuration → brief crew
        action plan → reassess with fresh telemetry. This loop repeats quickly and deliberately until the mission is
        back inside safe limits or a contingency path is committed.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
